---
// X-home — the two-state landing (DSGN-03 / DSGN-04, 00-RESPONSIVE-CONTRACT §5).
//
// WHAT WAS ASKED FOR, VERBATIM:
//   "just the homepage landing shows the photos section and prompts user to
//    scroll. as scrolled, the photo section moves fully up and only work+resume
//    sections are visible in the view"
// and, from the same message, the constraint that bounds every decision here:
//   "the sections for photos, work, resume as they exist on home right now stay
//    as is."
// Nothing is removed. No route collapses. No section is cut to make a budget.
//
// THE MECHANISM IS A PLAIN DOCUMENT SCROLL — not a scroll-driven animation, not
// a JS-pinned section. State A is one viewport tall, state B follows it in DOM
// order, and the prompt is a real anchor. That alone delivers every clause of
// the sentence, and it does so for four reasons the contract sets out:
//   1. it is the MINIMUM that satisfies the requirement — nothing in the
//      sentence asks for an animation;
//   2. it ships ZERO framework JS, which PUB-14 requires of four of five public
//      routes and check-no-js.sh gates;
//   3. it cannot trap a keyboard user, because there is nothing to trap;
//   4. it is ASPECT-RATIO-INDEPENDENT. The mechanism references exactly two
//      things — a viewport-height unit and DOM order — and neither is a function
//      of aspect ratio. That is the whole answer to the near-square foldable
//      (§5.2): the design is robust at aspect ~1.1 because it was specified in
//      viewport-height units and document order rather than in aspect ratio.
//      Any implementation reaching for `aspect-ratio` or a vw-derived height
//      forfeits that, which is why §1 forbids aspect-ratio branching.
//
// R-2, STATED WHERE THE NEXT READER WILL LOOK FOR IT. "Photos moves fully up" is
// the exact, enforceable half of the sentence and it is measured at all six
// classes. "Only work + resume are visible" describes what FILLS the view at the
// end of the transition — it is NOT a promise that both sections fit inside one
// viewport. With five projects (D-38) plus a resume section that is unachievable
// at every one of the six classes including 1440x900, and honouring the strict
// reading would mean cutting projects or the resume section from Home, which the
// user explicitly ruled out in the same message. State B continues below the
// fold at all six classes; that is the resolution, not a defect. (§5.7)

import { Eyebrow, Heading, Link, Text } from "@akhil-saxena/design-system";
import Public from "../layouts/Public.astro";
import HomeAct2 from "../components/HomeAct2.astro";
import home from "../data/home_config.json";
import manifest from "../data/portfolio_images.json";
import resume from "../data/resume.json";

// Accent colour. Ivory->Charcoal: the design system's `--amber*` family is never
// redeclared under charcoal, so `tone="accent"` and Link's default `inline`
// variant both render DS yellow (#fbbf24) on this page rather than the charcoal
// ochre. Recorded upstream finding; the local move is the legacy `color` prop,
// which is what home-act2.astro already does.
const OCHRE_D_STRONG = "var(--ochre-d-strong)";

const photos: Array<{
	id: string;
	title: string;
	urls: Record<string, string>;
	dimensions?: { width: number; height: number };
}> = Array.isArray(manifest) ? manifest : ((manifest as any).photos ?? (manifest as any).images);

// R-6 — REFLOW, NEVER HIDE. All six peekIds render at all six classes. Dropping
// to four on a narrow screen means two of the six photographs Akhil chose do not
// exist on that class, and a portfolio has no summary edition to fall back to.
// Column count and tile aspect are the variables below; tile COUNT is not.
//
// Resolved eagerly and loudly rather than with `?.` — a peekId that falls out of
// the manifest must name itself here, not render five tiles and look deliberate.
// This is the same failure shape X-home-act2 guards against for its five
// projects, and the same remedy.
const DEFAULT_FOCAL = "50% 50%";
const peek = home.peekIds.map((id) => {
	const p = photos.find((x) => x.id === id);
	if (!p) {
		throw new Error(
			`X-home: home_config.peekIds names "${id}", which is not in ` +
				`portfolio_images.json. All six peek photographs render at all six device ` +
				`classes (R-6) — this cannot degrade to five.`,
		);
	}
	return {
		id,
		title: p.title,
		small: p.urls.small,
		medium: p.urls.medium,
		thumb: p.urls.thumb,
		width: p.dimensions?.width,
		height: p.dimensions?.height,
		// ONE STORED FOCAL POINT, AND IT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS THE PER-PHOTO FIELD.
		// home_config.peekPositions holds exactly one entry
		// (architecture-hawamahaldaytime -> "50% 25%") and the other five default
		// to centre. The string is a CSS `object-position` value, which is
		// deliberately the same shape plan 00-19's schema decision 6 gives the
		// per-photo `focalPoint` field — so the two do not diverge into two
		// representations of one concept before Phase 3 has to migrate them.
		focal: (home.peekPositions as Record<string, string>)[id] ?? DEFAULT_FOCAL,
	};
});

if (peek.length !== 6) {
	throw new Error(`X-home: expected six peekIds, got ${peek.length}. See R-6 above.`);
}

// State B's second half. Small on purpose: its job is to be the second half of
// state B and to make §5.7's claim checkable, not to design /resume — that is
// Phase 5's route and this plan does not touch it.
// resume.json holds no prose summary field — it is experience / projects /
// skills / education, and nothing else. So the section states the shape of the
// record rather than paraphrasing it: inventing a summary sentence here would
// put copy on Home that exists in no fixture and that Phase 5 would then have to
// find an owner for.
const resumeCta = home.ctas.find((c) => c.link === "/resume") ?? home.ctas[home.ctas.length - 1];
const currentRole = resume.experience[0];
const roleCount = resume.experience.length;
const projectCount = resume.projects.length;
const skillHeads = resume.skills.map((s) => s.category).join(" · ");
---

<Public title="X-home — the two-state landing" active="home" maxWidth={1080}>
	<!--
	  STATE A. One viewport of photographs, with a prompt that is a real link.

	  The accessible name is an aria-label rather than a visible heading: §5.6
	  rule 6 requires each of the three sections to be a landmark with a name so
	  a screen-reader user gets the same navigation affordance the sighted user
	  gets from the prompt, and at class 3's narrow end every pixel of vertical
	  budget is spoken for. A label costs none.
	-->
	<section id="photos" class="hm-a" aria-label="Photographs">
		<div class="hm-name">
			<Heading
				level={1}
				size="4xl"
				weight="bold"
				className="hm-title"
				style={{ fontSize: "var(--hm-title)", lineHeight: "var(--lh-tight)" }}
			>
				{home.title}
			</Heading>
			<p class="hm-sub">{home.subtitle}</p>
			<p class="hm-tag">{home.intro}</p>
		</div>

		<ul class="hm-peek" role="list">
			{
				peek.map((p) => (
					<li class="hm-tile">
						<img
							class="hm-img"
							src={p.medium}
							srcset={`${p.small} 800w, ${p.medium} 1200w`}
							sizes="(max-width: 672px) 50vw, 33vw"
							width={p.width}
							height={p.height}
							alt={p.title}
							loading="eager"
							decoding="async"
							style={{ objectPosition: p.focal, backgroundImage: `url(${p.thumb})` }}
						/>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ul>

		<!--
		  THE SCROLL PROMPT IS A REAL ANCHOR TO A REAL TARGET (§5.6 rule 2). Not a
		  decorative chevron, not a div with a click handler, not a button calling
		  scrollIntoView. A keyboard user reaches state B with one Tab and one
		  Enter; it functions as a skip link; it works with zero JS; and it appears
		  in the accessibility tree as a named link to a named target.
		-->
		<Link href="#work" color={OCHRE_D_STRONG} className="hm-prompt">
			<span class="hm-prompt-label">Scroll for the work</span>
			<span class="hm-prompt-arrow">&#8595;</span>
		</Link>
	</section>

	<!--
	  STATE B. A plain wrapper, no landmark and no accessible name of its own —
	  the two sections inside it keep theirs, and adding a third region would put
	  a box in the rotor that names a framing the reader never encounters.
	-->
	<div class="hm-b">
		<!--
		  FIRST HALF — the work band. The single Act-2 composition, rendered from
		  the one component /home-act2 also renders, so the approved OQ-1 design
		  cannot exist twice in the screenshot record and disagree with itself the
		  first time either copy is touched.
		-->
		<HomeAct2 sectionId="work" headingId="hm-work-h" />

		<!--
		  SECOND HALF — the resume section. Real, named, and small.
		-->
		<section id="resume" class="hm-resume" aria-labelledby="hm-resume-h">
			<div class="hm-resume-head">
				<Heading level={2} size="3xl" weight="bold" id="hm-resume-h">The résumé</Heading>
				<Eyebrow size="sm" color={OCHRE_D_STRONG}>{`${projectCount} PROJECTS`}</Eyebrow>
			</div>
			<p class="hm-resume-now">
				{currentRole.role} at {currentRole.company}, {currentRole.period}.
			</p>
			<Text size="md" tone="muted" maxWidth={640}>
				{`${roleCount} roles and ${projectCount} projects. ${skillHeads}.`}
			</Text>
			<a class="hm-cta" href={resumeCta.link}>{resumeCta.text}</a>
		</section>
	</div>

	<Fragment slot="crosslink">
		<a class="hm-xlink" href="/photos" style={{ color: OCHRE_D_STRONG }}>
			all thirty-nine photographs →
		</a>
	</Fragment>
</Public>

<style>
	/* App LAYOUT css only. Nothing below restyles a design-system class, and no
	   colour below is anything but a charcoal token. */

	/* ═══ THE UNIT, AND THE THREE THAT ARE FORBIDDEN ═══════════════════════════
	   Settled by plan 00-21 in Public.astro's `body` rule and copied from there
	   rather than re-derived. Restated here because this is the file where the
	   choice actually bites.

	   THE THREE FORBIDDEN UNITS ARE NAMED IN FULL BELOW, EACH ON A LINE THAT
	   OPENS ITS OWN COMMENT. That is not a style tic. The acceptance gate for
	   this file is `grep -v '^[[:space:]]*[/*]' | grep -cE ...`, which drops a
	   line that OPENS a comment but keeps every continuation line inside it — so
	   a paragraph explaining why a unit was rejected reads to the instrument as
	   a declaration using it. Plan 00-21 hit this from the same direction and
	   the remedy is the same: keep the explanation, move the token onto a line
	   the filter can see is prose. Weakening the explanation to satisfy the
	   instrument would be the wrong repair. */

	/* `100vh` and `100lvh` resolve to the LARGE viewport — the height with the */
	/* mobile address bar RETRACTED. At first paint on iOS Safari and Android */
	/* Chrome the bar is VISIBLE, so that unit is taller than what the user can
	   see, and the ONE element whose entire job is to be seen at first paint —
	   the scroll prompt — is pushed below the fold. A two-state full-viewport
	   landing is precisely the design that unit breaks. */

	/* `100dvh` is FORBIDDEN on any scroll-transition participant, and this */
	/* section is exactly such a participant. It tracks the live viewport, so it
	   changes DURING the scroll and moves the transition's own target distance
	   mid-gesture — jitter on its own, and a fight with the snap point below.

	   `svh` is the smallest the viewport gets, so state A fits at first paint
	   under every chrome state. Desktop is unaffected: with no dynamic browser
	   chrome all four units are equal at classes 5 and 6, measured 900/900 at
	   1440x900 and 768/768 at 1024x768.

	   `min-height`, NEVER `height`. Content taller than the budget must overflow
	   VISIBLY and push the prompt down, rather than being clipped invisibly. A
	   visible overflow is a failure a screenshot catches; a clip is one it hides.
	   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

	/* HOW MUCH OF THE VIEWPORT STATE A DOES NOT GET, AND WHY IT IS A NUMBER.
	   State A is not the first thing on the page — the public shell puts the
	   AppBar row and .pub-main's top padding above it. If the section were a
	   full 100svh tall its BOTTOM would sit one viewport PLUS that chrome below
	   the top of the document, and one viewport of scroll would leave a band of
	   photographs still on screen. The requirement is that one viewport of
	   scroll clears state A COMPLETELY, so the chrome comes out of the budget —
	   which is also exactly how §5.2's own arithmetic counts it (its class-3
	   table spends "Nav 56" INSIDE the 712px it is budgeting).

	   MEASURED, not derived, at all six classes with navprobe22.mjs:
	     .pub-bar height  87px at 344, 390, 673, 768, 1024 and 1440 — CONSTANT,
	                      the AppBar does not wrap even at 344.
	     .pub-main pad-top 44px  = --space-11.
	     first section top 131px at all six classes.

	   87px IS A HARD-CODED MEASUREMENT AND THAT IS A DESIGN-SYSTEM GAP, NOT A
	   SHORTCUT. AppBar paints its own geometry and exposes no custom property
	   carrying its height, so a consumer building a full-viewport landing
	   underneath it has nowhere to read the number from. Recorded in
	   00-PUBLIC-DESIGN-NOTES.md. It is not left to rot: the departure assertion
	   in audit22.mjs measures state A's bottom edge against the viewport at all
	   six classes, so if AppBar's height ever moves, the gate goes red rather
	   than the landing going quietly wrong. */
	/* THE PAGE'S THREE MEASUREMENTS LIVE ON THE DOCUMENT ELEMENT, NOT ON STATE A,
	   AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS — IT IS THE ONLY PLACE THEY REACH FROM.

	   Astro scopes a page's styles with the PAGE's cid, and a component renders
	   with its OWN. The work band is components/HomeAct2.astro, so it emits
	   `data-astro-cid-53zdqmy2` while every rule written in this file compiles
	   to `data-astro-cid-vbfttlze` — and a bare `#work { }` here silently
	   matches nothing. It was written that way first and the audit caught it:
	   `getComputedStyle(work).scrollSnapAlign` read `none` while the source said
	   `start`. That is plan 00-21's rail defect exactly, in a new costume, and
	   the grep gates pass the broken version in both cases. Custom properties
	   defined here INHERIT into the component; the `:global` selectors below
	   REACH it. Both are scoped by `:has(.hm-a)` rather than left bare, so
	   nothing escapes onto another route no matter how the bundler chunks CSS. */
	:global(html:has(.hm-a)) {
		--hm-nav: 87px;
		--hm-above: calc(var(--hm-nav) + var(--space-11));
		--hm-sticky-nav: 0px;
	}

	.hm-a {
		min-height: calc(100svh - var(--hm-above));
		box-sizing: border-box;

		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-8);
		padding-bottom: var(--space-8);

		/* --hm-title carries the name treatment's size so the DS Heading can read
		   it through its `style` prop. Heading's TOKEN size scale stops at 4xl
		   (44px) and the handoff's name treatment is the 60px step, so the size
		   has to come in as a value; routing it through one custom property keeps
		   the per-class steps below in the stylesheet with everything else rather
		   than scattered across three inline styles. */
		--hm-title: var(--text-4xl);
	}

	@media (min-width: 673px) {
		.hm-a {
			--hm-title: var(--text-5xl);
		}
	}

	.hm-name {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-2);
		justify-items: center;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.hm-sub {
		font-family: var(--display);
		font-style: italic;
		font-size: var(--text-xl);
		line-height: var(--lh-snug);
		color: var(--ink-3);
		margin: 0;
	}

	.hm-tag {
		font-family: var(--display);
		font-size: var(--text-lg);
		line-height: var(--lh-snug);
		color: var(--ink-2);
		margin: 0;
	}

	/* ═══ THE PEEK GALLERY IS A GRID AT EVERY CLASS, NEVER A HORIZONTAL RAIL ═══
	   A rail fits any height budget, which is exactly what makes it tempting
	   here. It is forbidden on Home, and the reason is the CONTAINER: a rail
	   nests a horizontal scroller inside the vertical snap container this page
	   declares at the bottom of this stylesheet, and on iOS a horizontal rail
	   readily steals the vertical gesture the whole landing depends on.

	   The Photos filter row IS a rail at classes 1-2 (plan 00-21, §2) precisely
	   BECAUSE /photos has no vertical snap container. Same pattern, opposite
	   verdict, and the reason is the container — not the width, not the taste.
	   That file is one directory away and looks reusable; it is not reusable
	   here. ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

	/* STATE A IS A HEIGHT BUDGET (§5.3), AND THE NAIVE REFLOW INSTINCT IS
	   BACKWARDS. "Narrower viewport -> fewer columns" applied to a six-tile grid
	   makes it TALLER: fewer columns means more rows. So the column count steps
	   on the settled width rungs, and the tile ASPECT steps on a HEIGHT rung,
	   because the thing being solved is a height.

	   The height rung is NOT an aspect-ratio branch (§1 forbids those, and the
	   mechanism above stays aspect-independent because of it). It is a query on
	   the one axis the budget is denominated in, which is what §5.3 asks for in
	   as many words: "column count is a function of available height as much as
	   of available width".

	   16px gaps are --space-4, the same value X-home-act2 snapped the handoff's
	   off-grid 14px to, so the two peek grids agree. */
	.hm-peek {
		--hm-tile: 16 / 9;

		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: var(--space-4);
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		width: 100%;
	}

	/* classes 3-6 — three columns. Two columns of a 609px content width would be
	   three rows of 296px-wide tiles: 623px of gallery against the ~255px class
	   3's narrow end has left, which is not close. */
	@media (min-width: 673px) {
		.hm-peek {
			grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		}
	}

	/* THE HEIGHT RUNG. Below it the viewport cannot pay for 3:2 and the tiles go
	   16:9; at or above it 3:2 fits and the photographs keep more of themselves.
	   800px is where the arithmetic crosses, not a round number picked for
	   looking like one: at 1024x768 three 3:2 tiles are 301x201, so two rows plus
	   the gap are 418px, and 418 + the ~246px of name, prompt and padding is
	   664px against the 637px that 768 - 131 leaves. It does not fit. At 900 it
	   does. The rung sits between the two, and the departure assertion measures
	   both. */
	@media (min-height: 800px) {
		.hm-peek {
			--hm-tile: 3 / 2;
		}
	}

	.hm-tile {
		aspect-ratio: var(--hm-tile);
		border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
		overflow: hidden;
		/* Exception 5, carried from /photos: an inset one-pixel ring in DARK ONLY.
		   This gallery skews dark — architecture at night, "Into The Mist", the
		   wildlife set — and a dark-toned photograph bleeds into #161616 without
		   it. On a near-white field the page already draws the boundary. */
		position: relative;
	}

	:global(html.dark) .hm-tile {
		box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--rule);
	}

	.hm-img {
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		display: block;
		object-fit: cover;
		/* object-position comes in per tile from home_config.peekPositions. */
		background-size: cover;
		background-position: center;
	}

	/* THE PROMPT SITS AT THE BOTTOM OF THE BUDGET. `margin-top: auto` inside the
	   flex column pins it to the bottom edge of the one-viewport box, which is
	   where a scroll prompt has to be, and — because the box is min-height —
	   content that overruns pushes it DOWN and off the fold visibly instead of
	   clipping it. That is the failure mode being chosen deliberately. */
	.hm-prompt {
		margin-top: auto;
		align-self: center;

		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-2);

		font-family: var(--font-body);
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: var(--ls-wide);
		text-transform: uppercase;
	}

	.hm-prompt-arrow {
		font-size: var(--text-md);
		line-height: 1;
	}

	/* THE 44px FLOOR GOES ON THE HIT AREA, NOT THE PAINT, and it is gated on
	   pointer type rather than on width (§2) — a 1024px tablet is a coarse
	   pointer and a 1024px laptop is not, so width cannot answer this. The
	   padding grows the anchor's box; the words inside it do not move. */
	@media (pointer: coarse) {
		.hm-prompt {
			min-height: 44px;
			padding: 0 var(--space-3);
		}
	}

	/* ═══ STATE B ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
	   The work band is X-home-act2's own component and carries its own styles.
	   What belongs here is the anchor target's landing offset and the resume
	   section. */

	/* SCROLL-MARGIN-TOP IS A PER-CLASS NUMBER, AND ON THIS SHELL IT MEASURES
	   ZERO AT ALL SIX CLASSES. §5.6 rule 3 requires the anchor target to clear a
	   sticky nav, "and if the public nav is not sticky at a given class, the
	   value is 0 there". Measured with navprobe22.mjs: .pub-bar and the AppBar
	   inside it both compute `position: static` at 344, 390, 673, 768, 1024 and
	   1440 — the nav scrolls away with the page, so the work heading lands at the
	   viewport top with nothing over it and any positive value here would open a
	   gap instead of closing one.

	   It is declared as a property rather than left out because the value is a
	   function of the shell, not of this page: the moment PUB-09 or Phase 5 makes
	   the nav sticky, this is the one line that has to change, and a rung that
	   already exists is a much smaller change than a rule that does not.

	   It also has to be right for a second reason — scroll-margin shifts a SNAP
	   position as well as an anchor landing, so a wrong value here would move the
	   snap point declared below by the same amount. */
	:global(html:has(.hm-a) #work),
	:global(html:has(.hm-a) #resume) {
		scroll-margin-top: var(--hm-sticky-nav);
	}

	/* STATE B IS AT LEAST ONE VIEWPORT TALL, AND THIS RULE COMES STRAIGHT OUT OF
	   THE USER'S OWN SENTENCE rather than out of the test that caught it.

	   "as scrolled ... only work+resume sections are visible in the view". For
	   the work band and the resume section to be what FILLS the view once state
	   A has gone, they have to be able to fill it. Where they are shorter than a
	   viewport the reader lands on work, resume, the crosslink, the footer and a
	   band of nothing — which is not what was asked for.

	   IT WAS A REAL FAILURE AT EXACTLY ONE CLASS, AND ONLY A BROWSER FOUND IT.
	   Measured before this rule existed: at 768x1024 the whole of state B —
	   work, resume, crosslink and footer together — came to 1012px against a
	   1024px viewport. Twelve pixels short. The consequence is not cosmetic: the
	   document was 2036px tall, so its maximum scroll offset was 1012px, and a
	   page that cannot scroll a full viewport cannot complete the departure. The
	   audit read `scrollY=1012, photosBottom=12, NOT DEPARTED`. Tablet portrait
	   is the only class in the matrix tall enough in absolute pixels to run out
	   of document before it runs out of viewport; the other five clear it by
	   between 71px and 563px.

	   `svh` for the same reason state A uses it, and for one more: state A and
	   state B are the two halves of one transition, and denominating them in
	   different units would make the transition's target distance depend on
	   which half you measured. */
	.hm-b {
		min-height: 100svh;
	}

	/* ═══ SNAP IS AN ENHANCEMENT OVER THE PLAIN SCROLL, NEVER THE MECHANISM ════
	   §5.4 adopts exactly one form, and every clause of it is load-bearing.

	   `proximity`, and the contract FORBIDS the strict alternative. State B is
	   two sections and is taller than the viewport at every class, and under the
	   strict value the browser can snap back past content the reader is trying
	   to reach. `proximity` only pulls near a snap point, and there are two.

	   TWO SNAP POINTS, BOTH REACHABLE: the landing and the work band at the top.
	   Note the scroll-margin on the photos section — WITHOUT IT THE PAGE SNAPS
	   ON LOAD. A snap area starts at the element's own box, and state A's box
	   begins 131px down the document, below the AppBar row. That would put a
	   snap position at 131 rather than at 0, close enough to the initial scroll
	   offset for proximity to pull it, and the page would scroll itself by 131px
	   at first paint and hide the nav — precisely the "large involuntary
	   viewport translation" this whole block is written to avoid. Outsetting the
	   snap area by the chrome above it puts snap point one at 0, where the
	   landing actually is. Snap point two needs no outset because the work band
	   starts exactly one viewport down and the nav is not sticky.

	   IT IS VERIFIED IN A BROWSER, NOT BY A GREP. Plan 00-21 shipped a rail that
